  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 for a step function h. The current solution uses gradient-based optimization with multiple starting points and smooth approximations. We propose enhancing this by leveraging the structure of h and employing a more aggressive optimization strategy.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe objective is to minimize:\n    C\u2085 = max_k [ \u03a3 h[i] (1 - h[(i + k) % n]) ] * dx\nsubject to 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u03a3 h[i] = n/2.\n\nWe introduce a convex relaxation by approximating the max with a smooth function and enforce constraints via projection.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize h using provided initial_h_values.\nb. Perform gradient descent with adaptive learning rates.\nc. Use sequential quadratic programming for local refinement.\nd. Project onto the feasible set after each step.\ne. Track best solution and adapt optimization parameters dynamically.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nStart with n=100. Use two smoothing parameters and adaptive step sizes. Include momentum in gradient steps.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nThe budget allows for thorough exploration. Adapt learning rates and terminate early if no improvement.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nEnsure h is feasible and within constraints. Validate against original evaluator to confirm C\u2085 improvement.",
